/**

\page introduction_40

Peano's core is a plain C++ application using MPI plus a shared memory
library. It can add support for HDF5, vtk, and so forth; depends how
your code is configured. These underlying libraries/APIs are hidden away
from user code by the core. At the same time, we provide a Python API
which hides away most of the core. At one point, you will however be
required to program  directly with C++.

In the following sections, I run through some of the C++ techniques and
features of . Many users will never see the majority of them, as they
are hidden away---in particular if you work with high-level wrappers
around  such as . However, some low-level stuff is interesting for
everybody: How to use the logging and tracing, what support of unit
tests does exist, or how to program dimension-generic loops. Other stuff
is more techi, and serves more as a documentation guideline for core
developers. Yet, they also provide rationale why certain things in
 behave the way they do, once you have a large application up and
running.

*/